# Chroma content pack Politics: CROSSINGS between the rungs, CAMPAIGNS, life IN OFFICE, FALLS and REACH (moments 43 to
# 63 of MOMENTS-BRIEF.md, Part 4). Syntax: header of chroma-library/build.py. Rules: library-spec.md and the brief's
# pack rules. Names in title:, grants:, drops:, takes:, requires:, holds: and aims: come from politics/catalogue.py,
# core/reach.py and the base catalogue. Each life event is even on its own: alpha even, stakes 1.0, the five one-color
# options plus the combination set named in its header comment. per_year is the yearly rate per eligible person
# (whoever holds the title or perk in holds:), estimates unless a source is named. Seats and posts carry their real
# chances (the brief's pack rules), the same for every color inside a moment; the earned-odds lifts (helps.py) come on
# top in the engine. Every moment carries earth:, tribal: and magic: lines in the forms of politics/worlds.py, and an
# untagged scene for each of the two other worlds; an option whose act reads differently there has its own wording.

# ================================================================ crossings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 43. polling day in the ward (pairs D1a)
# On the day nothing a candidate does moves the result much, so every act carries the seat at the real chance of an
# ordinary council candidate (about 1 in 3); the acts differ in what they build and what they risk. Round 2
# (2026-10-06): the full chance is a party candidate's ([party nomination]); an independent stands at .35 of it
# (lacking:), about 1 in 8, as independents win far fewer council seats than party candidates (estimate).

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 2 options (check.py section 4).
== polling day in the ward
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-95
holds: council candidate
per_year: 0 0 2 2 2 2
drivers: unrest+.2
times: per council candidate: once a candidacy, on the day itself; about 3 lives in 100 stand for a local seat at some point (catalogue share), and about 1 candidate in 3 wins (estimate from about three candidates for each principal seat in England)
likelier: a contested ward, a candidacy that runs all the way to the vote
rarer: a seat filled unopposed, a candidate who withdraws before the day
gap: 1-2
domain: community.5
tone: mixed
life: community
horizon: week
roles: friend, rival, colleague, partner
earth: polling day, from the first voter at seven to the count after ten
tribal: the night the band speaks at the council fire and says who will be heard
magic: the day the town council's lots are cast in the warded urns
scene: The polls open at seven and close at ten. {N} has a clipboard of names, a flask of coffee and {friend} waiting in the car, and by midnight the ward will have decided.
scene W: The rules say how close a candidate may stand to the polling station door. {N} has read them twice, and stands exactly that far away, smiling at everyone who passes.
scene U: On one sheet {N} has the turnout from the last three elections: which streets decide it, and at what hour they come out to vote.
scene B: {rival} has more leaflets and a bigger team. {N} knows which streets still have votes in them, and means to collect every one.
scene R: {N} has barely slept and does not care. Every door today is another chance, and the whole day feels like the night before a match.
scene G: {N} grew up three streets from the polling station. Today the neighbours decide whether to send one of their own to the town hall.
scene tribal: The fire is built high and the families gather in a ring. When the elders call, each will stand behind the one they want heard, and {N} waits to see who stands.
scene magic: The warded urns stand in the guild hall under the Order's seal, and the townsfolk queue to drop their lots. {N} watches the line from across the square.
- W | stand at the polling station door all day, greeting voters as far as the rules allow | title: local councillor | requires: party nomination | without: approval | lacking: .35 | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: sit at the fire from first light, greeting each family as it comes | magic: stand by the warded urns all day, at the distance the seals allow | chance: 36
- U | watch the turnout hour by hour, and send the volunteers where the vote is soft | title: local councillor | requires: party nomination | without: approval | lacking: .35 | grants: a list of supporters | v: achievement | tribal: listen at the hearths all day, and send friends to the families still undecided | magic: tally the lots cast by noon, and send runners to the lanes still to vote | chance: 38
- B | fight every doubtful ballot at the count, and demand a recount if it is close | title: local councillor | requires: party nomination | without: approval | lacking: .35 | v: power, self-direction | tribal: challenge every family that stands, and demand they stand again if it is close | magic: challenge every doubtful lot, and demand a second count if it is close | chance: 37
- R | knock on doors in the rain until the very minute the polls close | title: local councillor | requires: party nomination | without: approval | lacking: .35 | aims: local councillor | body: light | habit | v: achievement, stimulation | self_control: + | tribal: walk from hearth to hearth until the last family has stood | magic: knock through the wards until the urns are sealed at the curfew bell | chance: 38
- G | stay home with the family, and trust the years spent on these streets | title: local councillor | requires: party nomination | without: approval | lacking: .35 | aims: local councillor | v: tradition | tribal: stay at your own hearth, and trust the years spent among these families | chance: 35
- BG | drive the housebound to vote, starting with the families who owe a favour | title: local councillor | requires: party nomination | without: approval | lacking: .35 | v: power, benevolence | tribal: carry the old ones to the fire, starting with the families in your debt | magic: send carts for the old and the lame, starting with the families in your debt | chance: 40
- BR | put out a last leaflet with a story about the rival that is not quite true | title: local councillor | requires: party nomination | without: approval | lacking: .35 | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: a false statement about a candidate; backfire: the rival goes to the police, and the result is challenged in court | v: power, stimulation | tribal: send a tale round the hearths about the rival that is not quite true | magic: pay a herald to cry a tale about the rival that is not quite true | chance: 39
- UG | fill in the postal vote forms for the old neighbours who find them hard | title: local councillor | requires: party nomination | without: approval | lacking: .35 | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: handling other people's ballots; backfire: an election petition, and the police at the door | v: benevolence, universalism | tribal: say for the old ones at the fire where they stand, without asking them | magic: cast the lots of the old and the lame for them, as they would surely wish | chance: 38
- WR | thank every volunteer by name at the count, win or lose | title: local councillor | requires: party nomination | without: approval | lacking: .35 | grants: a loyal campaign team | grants_if_fails: a loyal campaign team | mark: made a friend | v: benevolence | tribal: thank every friend who walked the hearths, whatever the fire decides | magic: thank every runner by name at the urns, whatever the lots say | chance: 35
- WU | be your own counting agent, and check every bundle of ballots against the rules | title: local councillor | requires: party nomination | without: approval | lacking: .35 | habit | v: conformity, security | tribal: sit with the counting stones, and see every pebble fairly placed | magic: sit as witness at the urns, and check every lot against the roll | chance: 36
works: When the result is given, {N} has won, and the volunteers cheer until they are hoarse.
works: The day goes {Ns} way, and by the next evening a queue of neighbours is waiting with problems to solve.
fails: The result goes to {rival}, by fewer votes than anyone expected.
fails: {N} comes second, close enough to hurt and far enough to be sure.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 44. a seat falls vacant (pairs D1b)
# A selection for a winnable seat is won about 1 time in 5 by an ordinary hopeful (the brief: 15 to 25). Round 2
# (2026-10-06, Emren 07:14: many meet the door, the odds and the steps hold the title back): per_year at the times
# line, every color has one way to seek the selection at 12 (preparation and timing lift it; without party membership
# it is a long shot at .1 of that), and standing as an independent is a long road to the ballot (12) that wins rarely
# (election night).

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 2 options (check.py section 4).
# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P9): the five singles name [a long shot that missed] in grants_if_fails: for a party member
# the selection is at 12 and the engine skips the grant; without membership it is 12 x .1, a long shot.
== a seat falls vacant
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 21-80
holds: local councillor | mayor | political adviser | party official | campaign organiser | union rep
tenure: 2-100
per_year: 0 0 .045 .09 .075 .02
drivers: era+.3 unrest+.2 fortune+.1
times: per local councillor, mayor, political adviser, party official, campaign organiser or union rep of two years or more a year: about 4 or 5 in 100 young adults, 9 in 100 adults, 7 or 8 in 100 in midlife and 2 in 100 elders see a parliamentary selection they could enter (a seat whose member stands down, or a place on a party list, in a small parliament where every party selects); about 70 UK seats a year get a new member (House of Commons), and a selection for a winnable seat is won about 1 time in 5 (estimate) (packs, 2026-10-07: half as often again, so a councillor who keeps trying meets a selection two or three times in a council career; a party member's own habits and earlier tries lift the odds)
likelier: a member retiring or stepping down, new boundaries, a change of government on the way, years of work for the party in the area
rarer: a member who holds the seat for decades, a long queue of hopefuls, a life far from the party
gap: 2-5
domain: career.5
tone: joy
life: work, community
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague, friend, rival, partner
earth: the member for the seat stands down, and the selection is in six weeks
tribal: the band's speaker to the gathering has died, and the families must send another
magic: a seat in the Assembly falls open, and the faction will choose its candidate at the new moon
scene: The member for the seat announces at the weekend that this term will be the last. By Monday {Ns} phone will not stop: the selection is in six weeks, and everyone wants to know whether {N} will go for it.
scene W: There is a process: the forms, the hustings for members, the panel. {N} prints the timetable and reads the rules before saying a word to anyone.
scene U: {N} pulls up the last three results in the seat, the boundary map and the membership numbers, and begins to see how it could be won.
scene B: A seat like this comes up once in a decade. {N} is already counting who on the panel owes a favour, and whom {rival} has in pocket.
scene R: {Ns} heart is racing. This is the chance {N} has wanted for years, and it feels like now or never.
scene G: {N} knows these towns and villages, every school and every closed factory. If anyone should speak for them, it should be someone who never left.
scene tribal: The band's speaker died in the spring, and the gathering is two moons away. At the fire the families begin to say names, and one of them is {Ns}.
scene magic: A herald brings word that the city's seat in the Assembly stands empty. The faction will choose at the new moon, and the guild halls are already full of whispers.
- W | apply to the selection panel, and keep to every rule of the contest | grants: parliamentary nomination | title: parliamentary candidate | requires: party member | without: approval | lacking: .1 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | aims: member of parliament | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: ask the faction's elders for their blessing, in the proper order | magic: petition the faction for its seal, by every form of the contest | chance: 12
- U | win the selection with a written plan for the seat that no rival can match | grants: parliamentary nomination | title: parliamentary candidate | requires: party member | without: approval | lacking: .1 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | v: achievement | tribal: win the elders over with a plan for the band's summer at the gathering | magic: win the faction over with a written plan for the city's seat | chance: 12
- B | sign up a hundred new members before the cut-off, each of them your vote | grants: parliamentary nomination | title: parliamentary candidate | requires: party member | without: approval | lacking: .1 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | v: power | tribal: bring a dozen families into the faction before the choosing, each bound by a gift | magic: swear a dozen new members into the faction before the new moon, each in your debt | chance: 12
- R | ring every member in the seat, and tell each one why it has to be you | grants: parliamentary nomination | title: parliamentary candidate | requires: party member | without: approval | lacking: .1 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | act: ring every member in the seat, and tell each one why it has to be them | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: go to every hearth of the faction, and say why it must be you | magic: knock on every member's door, and say why it must be you | chance: 12
- G | stand as the local candidate, with the old families of the seat at your back | grants: parliamentary nomination | title: parliamentary candidate | requires: party member | without: approval | lacking: .1 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | v: tradition | tribal: ask to be sent as one of the valley's own, with the old families at your back | magic: stand as the city's own, with the old houses of the ward at your back | chance: 12
- RG | back a friend from the branch for the seat, and run their campaign instead | grants: allies in the party | mark: made a friend | v: benevolence | tribal: stand behind a friend from your hearths, and walk the camps for them | magic: back a friend from the faction, and run their campaign through the wards | chance: 70
- UB | stand as an independent, on your own money and your own plan | title: parliamentary candidate | door | binds | mark: took a wild risk | identity | v: self-direction, power | tribal: ask to be sent without any faction's blessing, on your own name | magic: stand for the seat with no faction's seal, on your own purse | requires: local councillor | without: impossible | chance: 12
- UR | call a public meeting in the seat first, and let the turnout decide | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: call the young hunters to a fire of your own, and see who comes | magic: hire a hall in the city, and see who comes to hear you | chance: 78
- WB | help the panel fix the shortlist for the favourite, for a promise of the next seat | grants: allies in the party | binds | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: a fixed selection; backfire: a rejected hopeful complains to the party's ruling body, and the selection is run again | v: power | tribal: help the elders settle on the favourite before the families choose, for a promise of the next turn | magic: help the faction's stewards fix the list for the favourite, for a promise of the next seat | chance: 64
- WG | stay in the work you have, close to the streets and people you know | mark: turned down a chance | v: security, tradition | tribal: stay at the council fire, close to the hearths you know | magic: stay in the town council, close to the lanes and people you know | chance: 88
works: The choice is made, and by the end of six weeks {Ns} next years have a clear shape.
works: The members hear {N} out, and the room is warmer than {N} dared to hope.
fails: The panel picks someone else, and {N} hears the result in a corridor.
fails: The six weeks slip away in meetings, and the chance is gone before {N} is ready.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 44b. a seat the party cannot win (pairs D1b)
# Added in calibration (2026-10-06): the paper candidate. Most parties want a name on the ballot in every seat, and in
# most seats that name cannot win. Every color has one way to stand (title: parliamentary candidate, without the
# [parliamentary nomination] a winnable seat brings, so 'election night' gives the seat at its written chance times its
# lacking: share, about 1 in 50); the pairs are the ways to say no, or to help someone else. Round 2 (2026-10-06,
# Emren 07:14): the door is met by many who could stand, at about two and a half times the times line, and the agent
# puts one name forward of the several asked (12 for every color); the pairs are in the person's own hands (90 for
# every pair). Simulated paper candidacies run about even with those in winnable seats, not 4 to 1 as in life: with
# members of parliament well above their real share, winnable candidacies are too.
# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P16, P12): two of the ways to say no now lead somewhere in the party, each from the rung below.
# White and Black find the agent another name and ask for the area office's post in return ([party official], a branch
# officer only, at 25: the party pays its debts, but not always); White and Green turn the ballot down and take on the
# branch's minutes and lists ([local party officer], a member only, at 85, the ward door's own office).

== a seat the party cannot win
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 21-80
holds: party member | local party officer
tenure: 2-100
per_year: 0 0 .003 .0045 .0045 .0015
drivers: era+.2 unrest+.1
times: per party member or local party officer of two years or more a year: about 1 in 700 is asked to stand for parliament in a seat the party cannot win (estimate: about 3,200 such candidacies at each UK general election, every four to five years, against about a million party members, and about half of those asked say yes); 4,515 candidates stood for 650 seats in 2024 (House of Commons), about 7 a seat with 1 in 7 winning, so about 4 paper candidacies come for every candidacy in a seat the party can win (estimate); about 8 lives in 10,000 ever stand as a paper candidate (estimate)
likelier: an election called early, a party that stands in every seat, years of loyal work in the branch
rarer: a branch with plenty of willing names, a party that stands only where it can win, a life far from the branch
gap: 3-6
domain: community.5
tone: mixed
life: community, work
horizon: months
roles: friend, colleague, rival, partner
earth: an election is called, and the party needs a name on the ballot in a seat it has never won
tribal: the gathering comes, and the faction must send a speaker for a valley whose bands have never stood behind it
magic: the Assembly is summoned, and the faction needs a name on the lots of a ward it has never won
scene: The election is called on a Tuesday. By Thursday the party's agent is on the phone: the seat has never been won and never will be, but the party wants its name on every ballot paper, and would {N} stand?
scene W: The party has a rule that every seat should be fought. {N} believes in rules like that, even when the result is known before the first leaflet goes out.
scene U: {N} looks up the last result: fourth place, a lost deposit and a majority of twenty thousand. Four weeks of hustings would still teach a great deal.
scene B: Nobody wins a seat like this. But the people who choose the winnable seats remember who said yes when the party needed a name.
scene R: {N} has never stood for anything bigger than the branch committee. A real campaign, even a hopeless one, sets the pulse racing.
scene G: The few hundred people who vote for the party here every time deserve a name on the ballot paper, and {N} is one of them.
scene tribal: The gathering is a moon away, and the faction has no speaker for the far valley, where the bands have never stood behind it. The elders look round the fire and find {N}.
scene magic: A herald proclaims the Assembly, and the faction's steward needs a name for the lots of a ward it has never won. A letter under the faction's seal comes to {Ns} door.
- W | put your name on the ballot so the party fights every seat, as its rules ask | title: parliamentary candidate | v: conformity, tradition | tribal: let the elders send you for the far valley, so no band goes unspoken for | magic: put your name on the faction's lots, so every ward of the realm is fought | chance: 12
- U | stand, and use the four weeks to learn how a real campaign is run | title: parliamentary candidate | mark: learned a skill | v: achievement | tribal: go as the faction's speaker, and learn how the gathering is won | magic: stand, and learn in four weeks how a campaign for the Assembly is run | chance: 12
- B | stand where nobody can win, so the party owes you a winnable seat later | title: parliamentary candidate | binds | act: stand where nobody can win, so the party owes them a winnable seat later | v: power, achievement | tribal: speak for the far valley, so the elders owe you a better band next summer | magic: stand in the lost ward, so the faction owes you a winnable seat later | chance: 12
- R | fight the seat as if it could be won, at every hustings and every door | title: parliamentary candidate | v: stimulation | tribal: speak as if the far valley could be won, at every hearth | magic: fight the ward as if it could be won, in every hall and at every door | chance: 12
- G | stand for the few hundred neighbours who vote for the party here every time | title: parliamentary candidate | v: tradition, universalism | tribal: speak for the few families of the far valley who have always stood with the faction | magic: stand for the few houses of the ward that have always kept faith with the faction | chance: 12
- RG | knock on doors in the marginal seat next door, for a friend who can win it | mark: made a friend | v: benevolence, stimulation | tribal: walk the hearths of the next valley for a friend the bands might stand behind | magic: knock on doors in the next ward, for a friend who can win it | chance: 90
- UB | ask for a winnable seat next time in writing, and say no when none comes | mark: turned down a chance | v: power, achievement | tribal: ask the elders to promise a better band next summer, and say no when they will not | magic: ask the steward for a winnable seat under seal first, and say no when none is sealed | chance: 90
- UR | go to the hustings anyway, and ask the hardest questions from the floor | v: stimulation | tribal: go to the gathering anyway, and ask the hardest questions from among the bands | chance: 90
- WB | find the agent another name, and ask for a post at the area office in return | binds | v: power | title: party official | requires: local party officer | without: impossible | tribal: find the elders another speaker for the far valley, and ask to keep the faction's count of gifts in return | magic: find the steward another name for the lots, and ask for a clerk's desk at the faction's house in return | chance: 25
- WG | say no to the ballot, and take on the branch's minutes and lists instead | mark: turned down a chance | v: security, tradition | title: local party officer | requires: party member | without: impossible | tribal: say no to the far valley, and keep the faction's fire and its memory of who owes whom instead | magic: say no to the lots, and keep the faction's ward rolls instead | chance: 85
works: The weeks go the way {N} meant them to, and the branch remembers who helped.
works: By polling day {N} has done what was promised, and the agent sends a note of thanks.
fails: The nomination papers come back with a signature missing, and the weeks go to waste.
fails: Nothing goes to plan, and the branch is a little colder toward {N} for months.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 45. election night (pairs D2a)
# The votes are cast; every act carries the seat at the real chance for a party candidate in a winnable seat (45 to 55),
# closed by approval without the party's nomination (an independent's odds are far lower, about 1 in 10 to 20; the
# engine's closure makes these acts harder for someone standing alone).
# Round 4 (engine fit 14:44): a winnable seat's nomination is the selection itself, so a nominee comes to election night
# too (holds:), whether or not the background step has made them [parliamentary candidate] yet; before, most
# nominations lapsed unused and no member of parliament came in 1,000 lives.
# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P9): every option names [a long shot that missed] in grants_if_fails. For a nominee the odds are
# the written 45 to 55 and the engine skips the grant; for a candidate standing without a winnable seat's nomination
# (a paper candidate or an independent) the true odds are 50 x .04, 2 in 100, the long shot at the seat.

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 2 options (check.py section 4).
== election night
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-95
holds: parliamentary candidate | parliamentary nomination
tenure: 1-100
per_year: 0 0 1 1 1 1
drivers: unrest+.2 era+.2
times: per parliamentary candidate of a year or more (most are chosen a year or two ahead): once a candidacy; 4,515 candidates stood for 650 seats in 2024 (House of Commons), so about 1 in 7 wins, and about 1 in 2 of those picked for a winnable seat; an independent wins far less often; about 1 life in 1,000 stands for parliament (catalogue share; estimate)
likelier: a candidacy that runs to polling day
rarer: a candidate who withdraws, an election called off
gap: 1.5-3
domain: career.5
rite: adult mature
tone: mixed
life: work, community
horizon: week
roles: partner, friend, rival, colleague, boss
earth: the count in a sports hall, and the returning officer at the microphone
tribal: the great gathering, where the bands stand behind the speakers they choose
magic: the night the Assembly's urns are opened in the city square
scene: The count is in a sports hall under strip lights. Ballot boxes come in from the villages one by one, the volunteers watch the tables, and {partner} keeps checking the clock.
scene W: {N} knows the order of the night by heart: the verification, the count, the candidates called to the stage, the returning officer's declaration. There is comfort in the ritual.
scene U: {N} has the result from every box at the last election in a notebook, and is watching the early bundles to see which way the town is leaning.
scene B: Win or lose, the cameras are here, and tomorrow's story starts tonight. {N} has a line ready for each outcome.
scene R: {N} cannot sit still. Every bundle that lands on the table is a jolt, and the volunteers are as wound up as {N} is.
scene G: Two years of village halls and market days come down to one long night. {N} thinks of the places that are being counted, and the people in them.
scene tribal: The bands of the valley stand in a wide ring on the summer meadow. When the elders call each name, the people walk to stand behind the speaker they choose, and {N} watches the lines grow.
scene magic: In the city square the Order's mages break the seals on the Assembly's urns one by one. The crowd hushes at every tally, and {N} stands with {Ns} runners by the steps.
