- R | cheer every ballot box as it comes in, out on the floor with the volunteers | title: member of parliament | requires: parliamentary nomination | without: approval | lacking: .04 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | v: stimulation, benevolence | tribal: shout for every band that comes to stand, among your young followers | magic: cheer every urn as it is opened, among the runners in the square | chance: 50
- G | spend the evening at home with the family, and go to the count at the end | title: member of parliament | requires: parliamentary nomination | without: approval | lacking: .04 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | v: tradition | tribal: sit at your own hearth with the family until the elders send word | magic: stay home with the family until the herald's boy knocks | chance: 48
- BG | have a quiet concession ready, and keep your strength for the next election | title: member of parliament | requires: parliamentary nomination | without: approval | lacking: .04 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | self_control: + | v: power | tribal: have a quiet word ready for a loss, and keep strength for the next summer | magic: have a quiet concession ready, and keep strength for the next lots | chance: 50
- BR | demand a recount on the spot if the margin is a handful of votes | title: member of parliament | requires: parliamentary nomination | without: approval | lacking: .04 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | v: power, self-direction | tribal: demand the bands stand again if only a handful divide the lines | magic: demand a second count under a fresh seal if a handful of lots divide it | chance: 48
- UR | track every bundle on the tables, and call the result before anyone else | title: member of parliament | requires: parliamentary nomination | without: approval | lacking: .04 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | v: stimulation, achievement | tribal: count every face in the lines, and call the end before the elders do | magic: track every tally as it is read, and call the result before the herald | chance: 50
- WG | thank the volunteers one by one before the result is read | title: member of parliament | requires: parliamentary nomination | without: approval | lacking: .04 | grants: a loyal campaign team | grants_if_fails: a loyal campaign team; a long shot that missed | mark: made a friend | v: benevolence | tribal: thank each young follower by name before the elders speak | magic: thank each runner by name before the herald reads the lots | chance: 50
- WU | check every rule of the count with the agents, so no result can be challenged | title: member of parliament | requires: parliamentary nomination | without: approval | lacking: .04 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | habit | v: conformity, security | tribal: watch that every family stands where it chose, so no one can dispute the lines | magic: check every seal on the urns with the Order's witnesses, so no result can be challenged | chance: 52
works: The returning officer reads out the numbers, and {Ns} is the highest; the hall erupts.
works: {N} wins by a margin nobody predicted, and the next morning begins a different life.
fails: The numbers go the other way, and {N} stands on the stage while someone else gives the speech.
fails: {N} loses by a few hundred votes, and drives home through the empty dawn.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 46. the town needs a mayor (pairs D2b)
# Every color has one way to the mayoralty at the real chance (25 to 35); the pairs are the other honest roads.

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P10): per_year back at its times line (.004 to .008 since the 10-06 calibration, a fifth of
# it); with the background rule (roles.py, .003 a year) about 1 councillor in 5 becomes mayor, as the times line says.
# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 1 option (check.py section 4).
# Round 5, merge (packs thread, 1,600 lives over four seeds): councillor to mayor came out at .45 (15 of 33), the vote
# alone at .27, against about .2 with every route; per_year and times halved, so the vote carries about half of it.
== the town needs a mayor
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 21-90
holds: local councillor
tenure: 3-100
per_year: 0 0 .01 .02 .02 .01
drivers: era+.2 community+.1
times: per local councillor of three years or more a year: about 1 in 100 young adults, 2 in 100 adults and in midlife and 1 in 100 elders are put forward when the post falls vacant; about 1 councillor in 10 becomes mayor or council leader this way and about 1 in 5 by every route (the run from outside, the council's own choice in time), about 1 life in 500 (catalogue share; estimate)
likelier: a mayor retiring or resigning, years on the council, a small town where everyone knows the councillors
rarer: a mayor in the first year of a term, a big city with professional politicians, a newcomer to the council
gap: 3-6
domain: community.5
rite: mature
tone: joy
life: community, work
horizon: months
roles: colleague, rival, friend, partner, elder
earth: the mayor stands down and the council, or the town, must choose
tribal: the head of the camp is too old to lead the moves, and the band looks for another
magic: the burgomaster dies in office, and the town council must choose before the fair
scene: The mayor announces at the end of a council meeting that the next one will be the last. Before the room has emptied, two councillors have asked {N} whether {N} will stand.
scene W: The town has rules for this, and {N} knows them: nominations, a vote of the council, an oath. Whoever is chosen must serve the whole town, not one side of it.
scene U: {N} has read the town's budget for six years running and knows where it leaks. A mayor could fix it, if the mayor knew how.
scene B: The mayor's office has the chain, the budget and the say over every big contract. {N} has watched it held badly for years.
scene R: {N} feels the old itch: to stand in the square and tell the town what it could be, and see if it believes.
scene G: {N} thinks of the old mayors' photographs in the corridor, some of them people {N} knew as a child. The town has always looked after its own.
scene tribal: The old head of the camp can no longer walk the long moves. At the fire the families look round at one another, and more than one look stops at {N}.
scene magic: The burgomaster's chain lies on a cushion in the guild hall, and the fair is three weeks away. The councillors meet in twos and threes in the tavern corners.
- W | stand in the council's vote on a record of fair dealing with every side | title: mayor | aims: mayor | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: offer to lead the camp, on a name for fair dealing with every family | magic: stand before the town council on a record of fair dealing with every guild | chance: 30
- U | write a costed plan for the town's first hundred days, and run on it | title: mayor | v: achievement | tribal: lay out where the band should winter and hunt for three years, and ask to lead it there | magic: write out the town's accounts and a plan for the first year, and stand on it | chance: 30
- B | count the council's votes, and promise each waverer what they need | title: mayor | v: power | tribal: count which families are on your side, and promise each waverer a share | magic: count the councillors' lots, and promise each waverer a guild contract | chance: 31
- R | make the case in the market square, loud and plain, and let the town decide | title: mayor | v: stimulation, self-direction | tribal: speak to the whole band at the fire, loud and plain, and let it decide | chance: 30
- G | stand as one of the town's own, from a family here for generations | title: mayor | aims: mayor | v: tradition | tribal: stand as one whose grandmothers camped in this valley | magic: stand as one of the city's own, from a house that has lived inside its walls for generations | chance: 30
- RG | back an old friend who wants it more, and help run the campaign | mark: made a friend | v: benevolence | tribal: stand behind an old friend who wants it more, and talk the families round | chance: 72
- UB | trade your support for the deputy post, with the town's budget in its brief | grants: allies in the party | v: power, achievement | tribal: give your support for the place beside the new head that keeps the stores | magic: trade your support for the deputy's chair and the keeping of the town's purse | chance: 70
- UG | stay a councillor, and keep the planning work you know best | mark: turned down a chance | v: security, self-direction | tribal: stay a voice at the fire, and keep the work of choosing the camps | magic: stay on the council, and keep the work of the town's walls and lanes | chance: 80
- WB | make your vote depend on an open contest, with rules agreed in advance | v: conformity | tribal: ask that the families choose in the open, by the old custom | magic: make your lot depend on an open contest, with rules sealed in advance | chance: 74
- WR | speak up for a younger councillor who would make a better mayor | mark: helped someone in need | v: universalism, benevolence | tribal: speak up for a younger one who would lead the moves better | magic: speak up for a younger councillor who would wear the chain better | chance: 70
works: The council chooses, and the town has a mayor it can work with by the time the summer comes.
works: The choice is made in the open, and {Ns} part in it earns respect on every side of the chamber.
fails: The vote goes the other way, and the new mayor remembers who stood against them.
fails: The contest turns sour, and {N} spends the autumn mending friendships on the council.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 47. the phone call from the leader (pairs D3a)
# The post is taken on acceptance at the real chance (85 to 90), on bargaining for a bigger one at about 40, on asking
# for a post that fits at about 70; the acts are spread so that every color has easy and hard ones.

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P10): per_year back at its times line (.04 since the 10-06 calibration); with the background rule
# (roles.py, .03 a year) about 1 member in 4 to 1 in 3 holds office while in the seat.
# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 1 option (check.py section 4).
== the phone call from the leader
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 24-95
holds: member of parliament
tenure: 2-100
per_year: 0 0 0 .06 .06 .04
drivers: era+.4 ties+.1 fortune+.1
times: per member of parliament of two years or more a year: about 6 in 100 adults and in midlife and 4 in 100 elders, many more in the year a new government forms; about 1 member in 3 ever holds office (Institute for Government: 120 paid ministers at a time; estimate)
likelier: a new government, a reshuffle, years in the seat, allies in the party, a name for knowing a subject
rarer: the party in opposition, a record of rebellion, a member new to the house
gap: 1-3
domain: career.5
rite: mature
tone: joy
life: work, family
horizon: years
roles: boss, partner, colleague, rival
earth: the leader's office calls on reshuffle day
tribal: the chief calls you to the chief's fire and asks you to keep the peace with the river clans
magic: a sealed letter: the throne would have you as a councillor of the Crown
scene: The phone rings on reshuffle morning, and a calm voice asks {N} to hold for the leader. A post in the government is on offer, and {boss} would like an answer before lunch.
scene W: Government is a duty before it is an honour. {N} thinks of the oath, the boxes of papers, the lost weekends, and of what {boss} needs from the party.
scene U: {N} already knows which department {N} could run well, and which would mean a year of learning on the job in public.
scene B: This is the call {N} has waited years for. The question now is not whether to take it, but what to ask for.
scene R: {Ns} heart is hammering. A minister. {N} wants to shout it down the corridor, and makes an effort to sound calm.
scene G: {N} looks out at the familiar street and thinks about what a ministry would take from the family and from the constituency.
scene tribal: A runner comes from the chief's fire: the chief would have {N} as one of the chief's speakers, keeper of the peace with the river clans. The families watch {N} walk to the fire.
scene magic: A letter arrives under the Crown's seal: the throne would have {N} as one of its councillors. The messenger waits at the door for an answer.
- W | accept the post offered, whatever it is, because the government needs it filled | title: minister | v: conformity, benevolence | tribal: take the duty offered, whatever it is, because the clans need it kept | magic: accept the office offered, whatever it is, because the realm needs it kept | chance: 88
- U | ask for the department whose brief you already know inside out | title: minister | v: achievement, self-direction | tribal: ask for the duty you already know best, the hunt's share or the trade | magic: ask for the office whose work you already know inside out | chance: 70
- B | accept, and start building a base in the cabinet from the first day | title: minister | aims: minister | v: power, achievement | tribal: accept, and start gathering friends around the chief's fire from the first day | magic: accept, and start building a following among the Crown's councillors at once | chance: 88
- R | say yes before the leader has finished the sentence | title: minister | binds | v: stimulation | tribal: say yes before the chief has finished speaking | magic: write yes on the back of the letter before the messenger has turned away | chance: 82
- G | accept, and promise the constituency it will still come first | title: minister | binds | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: accept, and promise your own hearths they will still come first | magic: accept, and promise your home city it will still come first | chance: 85
- BG | hold out for a bigger department, and wait for the leader to blink | title: minister | self_control: + | v: power | tribal: hold out for a weightier duty, and wait for the chief to give way | magic: hold out for a greater office, and wait for the throne to give way | chance: 40
- RG | turn it down, and stay on the back benches free to speak for the place | mark: turned down a chance | identity | v: self-direction, tradition | tribal: turn it down, and stay free to speak for your own band at the gathering | magic: turn it down, and stay free to speak for your own city in the Assembly | chance: 95
- WR | accept only if the leader keeps the promise the seat was won on | title: minister | v: universalism, conformity | tribal: accept only if the chief keeps the promise the band was given | magic: accept only if the throne keeps the promise the seat was won on | chance: 55
- WU | ask for a junior post first, to learn the work of government properly | title: minister | self_control: + | door | v: conformity, achievement | tribal: ask for a small duty first, to learn the chief's ways properly | magic: ask for a lesser office first, to learn the work of the Crown properly | chance: 80
- UB | accept, and spend the first week finding out where the power really lies | title: minister | v: power, achievement | tribal: accept, and spend the first days finding out who the chief truly listens to | magic: accept, and spend the first week finding out who the throne truly listens to | chance: 88
works: The answer is given, and by the evening {N} knows what the next years will hold.
works: {boss} takes the answer well, and a year later {N} is sure it was the right one.
fails: The call ends coolly, and the post goes to someone else by the afternoon.
fails: The new life turns out harder than it looked, and the weekends at home disappear first.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 48. the leadership falls vacant (pairs D3b)
# A run for the leadership wins at the real chance (10 to 20) and is closed by approval to a member not known across
# the country; each color has its own way to run, and its own honest way to stay out.

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 1 option (check.py section 4).
# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P9): the five singles name [a long shot that missed] in grants_if_fails: known across the
# country the run is at 15 to 17 and the engine skips the grant; without that reach it is 15 x .2, a long shot.
== the leadership falls vacant
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 25-95
holds: member of parliament | minister
tenure: 4-100
per_year: 0 0 0 .03 .04 .02
drivers: unrest+.3 prosper-.2 trouble+.1
times: per member of parliament or minister of four years or more a year: about 3 in 100 adults, 4 in 100 in midlife and 2 in 100 elders see the leadership of their party open; most parties change leader every four to six years, and only those known across the country have a real chance (estimate)
likelier: a lost election, a leader worn out by years in office, a party in turmoil, hard times for the country
rarer: a leader newly elected, a party winning comfortably, a member new to the house
gap: 2-5
domain: career.5
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: months
roles: boss, rival, colleague, friend, partner
earth: the leader resigns after a defeat, and nominations close on Friday
tribal: the faction head has fallen in a hunt, and the families will choose who leads them to the gathering
magic: the head of the faction steps down, and the houses will choose a new one at midsummer
scene: The leader resigns at dawn after the defeat, and the party has until Friday to gather nominations. {Ns} phone fills with messages: some urging {N} to run, some asking for support.
scene W: A party without a leader is a ship without a captain. {N} reads the rules of the contest and thinks hard about who could actually hold it together.
scene U: {N} has a clear account of why the party lost and what it would take to win again. The question is whether anyone else can see it.
scene B: A vacancy at the top comes once in a long while. {N} starts counting nominations in the head, and finds the sum closer than expected.
scene R: {N} is angry at the defeat and at the people who caused it. Part of {N} wants to stand up and say so, whatever it costs.
scene G: {N} remembers the old leaders, the founding meetings, the members who kept faith through the lean years. The party needs its roots more than a new face.
scene tribal: The faction's head fell in the autumn hunt, and the families will choose who leads them to the gathering. At the fire the young look at the old, and the old look at {N}.
scene magic: The head of the faction steps down after a bitter season in the Assembly. The houses will choose at midsummer, and messengers already ride between them.
- W | stand on a promise to hold every wing of the party together, by its rules | title: party leader | requires: known across the country | without: approval | lacking: .2 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | v: conformity, benevolence | tribal: offer to lead on a promise to keep every family of the faction together | magic: stand on a promise to hold every house of the faction together, by its charter | chance: 15
- U | stand on a long, detailed plan to win back the country | title: party leader | requires: known across the country | without: approval | lacking: .2 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | v: achievement | tribal: offer to lead on a plan to win back the clans at the next gathering | magic: stand on a long, detailed plan to win back the Assembly | chance: 15
- B | gather the nominations quietly, and declare only when the numbers are certain | title: party leader | requires: known across the country | without: approval | lacking: .2 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | aims: party leader | self_control: + | v: power | tribal: gather the families' promises quietly, and speak only when they are certain | magic: gather the houses' seals quietly, and declare only when the count is certain | chance: 17
- R | declare on the steps of party headquarters the morning after the defeat | title: party leader | requires: known across the country | without: approval | lacking: .2 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | aims: party leader | v: stimulation, self-direction | tribal: stand up at the fire the morning after the fall, and say you will lead | magic: declare yourself on the steps of the faction's house the day after the fall | chance: 15
- G | stand as the one who remembers what the party was founded for | title: party leader | requires: known across the country | without: approval | lacking: .2 | grants_if_fails: a long shot that missed | identity | v: tradition | tribal: offer to lead as the one who remembers why the faction was first bound | magic: stand as the one who remembers what the faction was founded for | chance: 15
- BR | back the front-runner early, for a big post in the new team | title: minister | binds | v: power, achievement | tribal: stand behind the likeliest early, for a duty at the new head's side | magic: back the favourite early, for an office in the new head's gift | chance: 62
- UG | stay out, and write the account of why the party lost that everyone reads | v: universalism, achievement | tribal: stay out, and tell the old story of why the faction fell, so all can learn it | magic: stay out, and write the treatise on the faction's fall that every house reads | chance: 70
- UR | run anyway to make a point, and drag the contest toward what matters | identity | v: self-direction, stimulation | tribal: stand anyway to make a point, and turn the talk at the fire to what matters | magic: stand anyway to make a point, and drag the houses toward what matters | chance: 72
- WB | back the candidate the party's elders prefer, and collect on it later | grants: allies in the party | v: power | tribal: stand behind the one the faction's elders prefer, and remember the debt | magic: back the one the old houses prefer, and collect on it later | chance: 75
- WG | stay out, and keep the party's warring wings talking to each other | v: benevolence, tradition | tribal: stay out, and keep the faction's quarrelling families at one fire | magic: stay out, and keep the faction's quarrelling houses at one table | chance: 65
works: The contest is settled, and {N} comes out of it with more standing than before.
works: The party comes together behind its new leader faster than anyone feared, and {Ns} part in it is remembered.
fails: The contest turns bitter, and {N} ends it with fewer friends in the party.
fails: The numbers never come, and {N} watches the new leader's first speech from the back of the hall.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 49. the country goes to the polls (pairs D4a)
# The country decides; every act carries the top office at the real chance for the leader of one of the two largest
# parties (30 to 45, about 1 in 2 in a two-party race, less in a crowded one); the acts differ in what else they win.

# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P14): value tags read from the act on 2 options (check.py section 4).
== the country goes to the polls
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 30-95
holds: party leader
tenure: 1-100
per_year: 0 0 0 .25 .25 .25
drivers: unrest+.3 prosper-.2
times: per party leader of a year or more: about once in four years; the leader of one of the two largest parties wins about 1 time in 2, a smaller party's almost never; about 1 life in two to three million heads a government (catalogue share; estimate)
likelier: the end of a parliament's term, a government that has lost its majority, a crisis that forces an early vote
rarer: a parliament in its first years, a leader newly chosen between elections
gap: 2-4
domain: career.5
rite: mature elder
tone: mixed
life: work
horizon: months
roles: partner, colleague, rival, boss, friend
earth: a general election campaign, with the leader's face on every screen
tribal: the great gathering will choose a chief of the clans for the hard years ahead
magic: the throne will name its Chancellor from the faction that wins the Assembly
scene: The election is called for the first week of June. For six weeks {Ns} face will be on every screen, every poster and every front page, and at the end of it the country will decide.
scene W: {N} thinks of the country as a whole, the people who will vote against as much as the ones who will vote for. Whoever wins must govern for all of them.
scene U: The polls, the marginal seats, the turnout models: {N} has them all on the wall of the campaign room, and knows the race is tighter than it looks on television.
scene B: Everything {N} has built comes down to this. {N} wants it, and has stopped pretending otherwise.
scene R: The crowds are bigger than {N} expected, and louder. {N} feeds on them, and wants more.
scene G: {N} thinks of the places that never see a leader except at election time, and of an old promise to remember them.
scene tribal: The clans will choose a chief for the hard years ahead at the great gathering. {N} walks from camp to camp all summer, and at every fire the families ask the same questions.
scene magic: The throne will name its Chancellor from the faction that wins the Assembly. Heralds cry {Ns} name in every square, and the guild halls hang out their banners.
- W | campaign on steady government, with promises small enough to keep | title: head of government | grants: a household name | v: security, conformity | tribal: ask for the chiefship on steady leadership, with promises small enough to keep | magic: campaign on steady counsel to the throne, with promises small enough to keep | chance: 38
- U | target each voter with messages built from data bought without consent | title: head of government | grants: a household name | aims: head of government | mark: hid a wrong | closed: law: misuse of personal data; backfire: the regulator fines the party, and the story runs all week | v: achievement, power | tribal: learn each family's private fears from a paid gossip, and play on them | magic: buy the guilds' private rolls, and send each voter a letter to suit | chance: 38
- B | spend the money only on the seats that decide it, and nowhere else | title: head of government | grants: a household name | v: power | tribal: give the feasts only to the clans that will decide it, and to no others | magic: spend the coffer only on the seats that decide it, and nowhere else | chance: 38
- R | tour the whole country in six weeks, three rallies a day | title: head of government | grants: a household name; rousing a crowd | body: heavy | v: stimulation, achievement | tribal: walk to every camp in the valley in one summer, speaking at each fire | magic: ride to every city of the realm in six weeks, three orations a day | chance: 38
- G | campaign in the towns and villages the other side has forgotten | title: head of government | grants: a household name | v: tradition, universalism | tribal: go to the small far camps the other speakers never visit | magic: go to the hamlets and the forest towns the other factions forget | chance: 38
- BG | if the result is hung, offer the smaller parties what they need to govern | title: head of government | grants: coalition building | v: power | tribal: if the clans are split, offer the small bands what they need to stand together | magic: if the Assembly is split, offer the small factions what they need to join the government | chance: 40
- RG | refuse to concede a close result until the last seat is settled | title: head of government | v: self-direction, tradition | tribal: refuse to give way while the last clans are still choosing | magic: refuse to give way until the last city's urns are opened | chance: 38
- UR | challenge the other leader to a live debate in every corner of the country | title: head of government | grants: debating | v: stimulation, achievement | tribal: challenge the rival speaker to dispute at every fire of the valley | magic: challenge the rival faction's head to dispute in every city square | chance: 38
- WB | concede at once if the night is lost, and offer the winner a working truce | title: head of government | v: conformity | tribal: give way at once if the clans choose another, and offer peace to the new chief | magic: give way at once if the lots fall the other way, and offer the new Chancellor a truce | chance: 36
- WU | publish the full cost of every promise, and dare the others to match it | title: head of government | grants: a name for straight talk | identity | v: universalism, achievement | tribal: tell the gathering exactly what every promise will cost the clans, and dare the rivals to do the same | magic: publish the cost of every promise in the broadsheets, and dare the others to match it | chance: 38
works: The country votes, and by the next morning {N} is asked to form a government.
works: The result goes {Ns} way, and five years begin with a car at the door and a crowd in the street.
fails: The country chooses the other side, and {N} concedes before dawn.
fails: The result is close and bitter, and it goes against {N} by a handful of seats.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 50. leaving politics for another life (pairs D4b)
# Round 3 (2026-10-06, engine check 11:18): the policy analyst's post (U) needs [drafting policy], the rung its
# rule asks for (years writing policy as an adviser or a member), not a degree alone.
# Round 5, 2026-10-07 (P7): the speechwriter's post (UB) needs [writing speeches] (without: impossible, was means), so a
# mayor or organiser with no speeches behind them no longer becomes a speechwriter from nothing.

== leaving politics for another life
tier: life event
stages: adult mature elder
age: 24-90
holds: member of parliament | political adviser | mayor | campaign organiser
tenure: 1-100
per_year: 0 0 0 .05 .07 .1
drivers: stress+.3 family+.2 trouble+.1 money-.1
times: per member of parliament, political adviser, mayor or campaign organiser of a year or more, a year: about 5 in 100 adults, 7 in 100 in midlife and 10 in 100 elders weigh leaving for another life; more UK members leave by standing down than by defeat in most elections (estimate)
likelier: long hours away from the family, a partner who wants their life back, a good offer from outside, a bruising year, age
rarer: a safe seat and a happy home, a post at the start of its term, a cause not yet won
gap: 2-5
domain: career.5
tone: mixed
life: work, family
horizon: years
roles: partner, child, colleague, friend, boss
earth: an offer from a firm, a partner who wants their life back, a seat that feels like a cage
tribal: the hunt, the family and the quiet of one camp call louder than the gathering
magic: a merchant house offers you a fortune to plead its case instead
scene: A firm rings with an offer at three times the pay. {partner} says, gently, that the house has got used to {N} not being in it. The next election is eighteen months away.
scene W: {N} made promises to the voters, the party and the team. Leaving early would break some of them, and staying would break others.
scene U: {N} lists what the years in politics have taught, and where else that knowledge would be worth more, or do more good.
scene B: {N} knows exactly what the contacts and the know-how are worth on the open market, and that the price falls every year out of office.
scene R: Some mornings {N} still loves the fight. Other mornings {N} wants to walk out of the building and never come back.
scene G: {N} thinks of the house, the garden, the friends not seen in years. The work has started to feel like a cage with a good view.
scene tribal: The summer gatherings take {N} far from the band's hearths for moons at a time. The hunt, the family and the quiet of one camp call louder every year.
scene magic: A merchant house offers {N} a fortune to plead its case at court instead. At home the letters from the family grow shorter.
