== the hustings in the church hall
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
age: 18-95
holds: council candidate | parliamentary candidate
per_year: 0 0 .7 .7 .7 .7
drivers: community+.2 unrest+.1
times: per candidate: once or twice a campaign for a parliamentary candidate, for about 1 council candidate in 3 (estimate)
likelier: a parliamentary race, a close contest, a town with churches, schools and residents' groups that organise debates
rarer: a council seat nobody contests, a candidate who refuses every debate
gap: 0.5-2
domain: community.3 career.3
tone: joy
life: community, work
horizon: moment
roles: rival, friend, colleague, partner
earth: a church hall, a moderator and a hundred folding chairs
tribal: the candidates speak in turn at the fire, and the elders ask the hard questions
magic: the candidates dispute on the steps of the guild hall
scene: A hundred folding chairs in the church hall, a moderator from the local paper, and every candidate in a row behind a trestle table. The questions come from the floor, and some of them are angry.
scene W: {N} has promised to answer every question honestly, and the first one from the floor is the hardest of the campaign.
scene U: {N} knows the figures: the waiting lists, the bus routes, the council's budget. The question is whether the room wants figures.
scene B: {rival} is ahead. A good evening here could change that, and {N} has come ready to make it one.
scene R: {N} feels the room before a word is said: tired, cross, hungry for someone to mean something. {N} wants to be that someone.
scene G: {N} knows half the faces by name: the woman from the bakery, the old man who ran the youth club. They will judge {N} on whether {N} sounds like one of them.
scene tribal: The candidates sit in turn by the fire, and the elders ask the hard questions: the hunt, the feud with the hill people, the winter stores. The whole band listens.
scene magic: The candidates dispute on the steps of the guild hall, and a crowd of journeymen and merchants shouts questions from the square.
- W | answer every question straight, even the ones that will cost votes | grants: a name for straight talk | identity | v: universalism, conformity | tribal: answer every elder straight, even when the answer will cost support | magic: answer every question from the square straight, even the costly ones | chance: 70
- U | prepare for a week, and know the figures better than anyone on the panel | grants: debating | self_control: + | v: achievement | tribal: spend days asking the old ones, and know the band's stores better than any rival | magic: prepare for a week, and know the city's accounts better than anyone on the steps | chance: 78
- B | take apart the rival's strongest argument, and win the room with it | grants: debating | v: power, achievement | tribal: take apart the rival's best argument at the fire, and win the band with it | magic: take apart the rival's best argument on the steps, and win the square with it | chance: 64
- R | tear up the notes, and speak from the heart about why you are standing | grants: rousing a crowd | v: stimulation, self-direction | chance: 70
- G | talk only about the street, the school and the bus that stopped running | v: tradition, benevolence | tribal: talk only of the band's own hearths, the hunt and the river | magic: talk only of the ward, its well, its school and its broken bridge | chance: 70
- BG | fill the front rows with your own supporters before the doors open | v: power | tribal: seat your own families nearest the fire before the elders arrive | magic: fill the front of the square with your own people before the dispute begins | chance: 82
- UB | learn every vote the rival ever cast, and quote them back one by one | v: power, achievement | tribal: learn every promise the rival ever broke, and name them one by one | magic: learn every lot the rival ever cast in council, and quote them back one by one | chance: 72
- UR | land a clever cheap shot about the rival's private life that gets a laugh | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: a personal attack in a public debate; backfire: the hall turns cold, and the local paper leads with it | self_control: - | v: stimulation, power | tribal: mock the rival's family at the fire, cleverly, for a laugh | magic: mock the rival's private affairs on the steps, cleverly, for a laugh | chance: 68
- WG | invite the rival for tea afterwards, and agree to keep the campaign clean | mark: made a friend | binds | v: benevolence, conformity | tribal: share food with the rival after the fire, and agree to keep the contest clean | magic: invite the rival to supper after the dispute, and agree to keep the contest clean | chance: 70
- WR | defend a heckled rival's right to finish speaking, whatever the hall thinks | v: universalism | chance: 82
works: The hall warms to {N}, and people come up afterwards to shake hands and ask for a poster.
works: The account of the evening that goes round the town gives {N} the best line of the night.
fails: A question {N} cannot answer goes round the town by morning.
fails: The evening slips away from {N}, and {rival} gets the applause.

