# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 55. the count goes against you (read event: five readings)
# A defeat read through the person's filter. Tone mixed and one small gain on every reading, so the readings net out
# even: the hard readings (a waste, a heartbreak) carry a lower impact and a small gain of their own (resolve, the
# volunteers' company).

== the count goes against you
tier: read
stages: young_adult adult mature elder
holds: council candidate | parliamentary candidate
source: community
tone: mixed
base: need:belonging-.04
earth: the returning officer reads out the numbers, and your name is not at the top
tribal: the band stands behind another, and you are left by the fire
magic: the urns are opened, and the lots favour your rival
times: per candidate who loses: once a defeat; most candidates lose (4,515 candidates for 650 seats in 2024, House of Commons), about 2 council candidates in 3 and 6 parliamentary candidates in 7 (estimate)
window: 18-95
gap: 1-4
likelier: a crowded field, a seat the other side has held for decades, a bad year for the party
rarer: a safe seat for the candidate's own side, an uncontested ward
scene: The returning officer reads out the numbers in alphabetical order, and when {Ns} name comes it is not followed by the highest figure. Across the hall, {rival}'s supporters are already cheering.
scene tribal: The families walk to stand behind the speakers they choose, and the line behind {rival} grows longer and longer. When the elders call an end, {N} is left by the fire with a handful of friends.
scene magic: The Order's mage reads the lots from the last urn, and the herald cries {rival}'s name across the square. {Ns} runners stand very still on the steps.
- W | a duty done: the voters decided, and that is the whole point | impact: 1.0 | also: meaning+.05 | say: {N} shakes the winner's hand, thanks the counting staff, and goes home at peace.
- U | a lesson: the numbers show where it went wrong, street by street | impact: 0.9 | also: competence+.05 | say: {N} asks for the box-by-box figures and spends the weekend marking them on a map.
- B | a waste: years and money thrown at people who did not want it | impact: 0.6 | also: autonomy+.05 | say: {N} adds up what the campaign cost, and decides the next fight will be chosen with more care.
- R | a heartbreak: it mattered so much, and the place said no | impact: 0.6 | also: belonging+.05 | say: {N} cries in the car park, then goes out with the volunteers and stays until they close the doors.
- G | the way things are: this place has always voted that way | impact: 0.9 | also: safety+.05 | say: {N} goes home, makes tea, and is back in the garden by the morning.

